es minutes.' Lucy Fairbanks stopped running. She turned around and went to find him."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Choice', 'text': "old heartbreak left town with the gift wrap and the almosts. Lucy Fairbanks felt nothing but pity — and then not even that, because her arms were full of present tense.\n\nShe found him at the shore, where it started. 'I choose,' she said, breathless, 'the thing that scares me. I choose you. I've always chosen you — I just took seventeen years to say it.'\n\nHe laughed through tears, the same laugh, and the sunset did its thing, and Rosewood held its breath again. This time nobody interrupted. This time they stayed."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'The Promise', 'text': "They danced on the porch to the mixtape — no, to a new playlist, because the past is a place to visit, not to live. Lucy Fairbanks had learned that at last.\n\nlove is worth the risk — Lucy Fairbanks wrote it in the new journal, the one for the next seventeen years. In Rosewood, under the stars, with love: the whole story, and only just beginning.\n\nLate that night, on the shore, they stood where the boy and girl had stood. 'We made it,' he said. 'We were always going to,' she said, and believed it, the heart knowing first."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Epilogue in Wildflowers', 'text': "Lucy Fairbanks walks the shore most evenings, sometimes alone, sometimes with him, always with Rosewood around her like a held breath. The sunsets haven't changed. She has — into someone who stays.\n\nAnd when the children ask about a mixtape, Lucy Fairbanks tells them: it was a map. Not to a place — to a person. To the bravest thing she ever did, which was to come home.\n\nher grandmother Rose still diagnoses everything with pie. Her grandmother Rose's quilt is worn thin with love. old heartbreak is a story they tell laughing, a ghost with no haunting left in it."}]}

